# Accepted occurrences of the network-reaching, destructive, and
# credential-shaped tokens that checks.conformance.check_network_prose counts in
# every SKILL.md — the module under internal/tests/unit/src/, run by the
# unit-tests CI job. The token set itself lives in
# checks.conformance.clauses.NETWORK_PROSE_PATTERNS and nowhere else; this file
# records only how many occurrences of it the repository currently accepts, and
# why.
#
# FORMAT  <path> <token> <count>
#
#   path   the skill body, relative to this file's own directory
#   token  the token as written in the script's set. A token containing a space
#          (`rm -rf`) is written literally: the path is the first field, the
#          count the last, the token everything between.
#   count  occurrences accepted in that body for that token
#
# Blank lines and everything from a `#` to end of line are ignored. A pair with
# no record here is accepted zero times, so a token appearing in a body the file
# does not mention fails the build.
#
# ADDING AN ENTRY. The check fails with the body, the token, the count it found,
# the count accepted, and every matching line. If the occurrence belongs in the
# skill, add a record with the measured count and, above it, a comment saying
# what the occurrence is and why it is acceptable. State the substance, not the
# fact of acceptance: a reviewer reading "curl 2" learns nothing, while a
# reviewer reading why those two occurrences are prohibitions can tell at a
# glance whether a third one is of the same character. Record no line numbers —
# prose in this repository is hand-wrapped and they move for cosmetic edits;
# quote or paraphrase the occurrence instead, which is also what makes a later
# substitution at the same count conspicuous.
#
# Every count here must EQUAL the occurrences present in the body it names, not
# merely be greater than or equal to them. The check fails only on an increase,
# so an over-stated count is invisible and silently grants one future occurrence
# acceptance without review.
#
# WHAT A RECORD HERE DOES NOT MEAN. Every occurrence below is a sentence or a
# declaration that FORBIDS the behaviour its token denotes, and no token match
# can tell "never run curl" from "run curl". A record is not evidence that the
# occurrence is safe; it is evidence that a human looked at it once.
# docs/internal/explanation/network-prose-gate.md has the rest.

# Two renderings of the example constraint `- No eval()`, one confirmed and one
# inferred, in the passage explaining how the skill treats a constraint the plan
# declares. Both are prohibitions the agent is told to honour, and the skill
# neither evaluates nor emits code from them.
research/ai-implement/SKILL.md eval 2

# Two prohibitions of arbitrary HTTP in the merged ai-scaffold-release body,
# both about denying it rather than doing it. One is the `Bash(curl:*)` entry in
# `disallowed-tools`, which the tool-declaration check above parses as a token
# list, so that occurrence is machine-verified to be a denial and not prose. The
# other is the sentence in the lead paragraph stating that the forge CLI and
# arbitrary HTTP are denied to the skill, so a promotion can only happen through
# the installed script or the job that runs it. This body configures a publish
# path and never performs one; a third occurrence would be the first that could
# be an instruction to fetch something, and belongs under review.
release/ai-scaffold-release/SKILL.md curl 2
